/**
@file structure.dox
@brief Directory documentation, so the generated site has a shape.

Doxygen's default output for this engine would be one flat list of hundreds of
declarations in `Vkm::Engine`, which is accurate and useless. These `@dir` blocks
give the site the structure the source tree already has, without putting an
`@ingroup` line on every declaration - a tax the source would pay forever to
serve the site.
*/

/** @dir src/engine/ecs
    @brief The ECS: entities, the open type-erased Scene, and every component.

    Open and registration-free. A component is any plain struct; the storage is
    one sparse set per type, and entity ids are generational so a stale one is
    caught rather than reused. */

/** @dir src/engine/ecs/component
    @brief The components the engine itself understands.

    Data only - no methods that do work, no base class. A system somewhere reads
    each of these; a game adds its own the same way. */

/** @dir src/engine/core
    @brief Foundations: the frame contract, events, math, memory, hashing.

    `System` and `FrameContext` live here, which is where the shape of a frame is
    actually defined. */

/** @dir src/engine/core/math
    @brief Vector and matrix helpers on top of glm, and the engine's axis convention.

    Ask an orientation through computeForward / computeRight / computeUp rather
    than writing an axis by hand. */

/** @dir src/engine/resource
    @brief Assets: owned once by ResourceManager, referenced by Handle, named for serialization.

    The name is the identity. A handle is a slot plus a generation and is only
    meaningful to the manager that minted it, so what a scene file stores is the
    name and what it resolves against is whatever manager is loading it. */

/** @dir src/engine/resource/generate
    @brief Procedural meshes, textures and materials a game can build at run time.

    Here rather than in the tools module because a project links the engine and
    not the tools: a game that generates its world needs a cube without importing
    one. */

/** @dir src/engine/io
    @brief Reading and writing: scenes, prefabs, the project file, cooked assets.

    Each format has a version gate of its own, and what it refuses differs by
    format; none of them grows a back-compatibility read path - see
    guides/engine.md section 5.1. */

/** @dir src/engine/system
    @brief The per-frame systems, one directory each.

    Each is registered at exactly one stage and talks to the others only through
    FrameContext. The stage order is Input, Simulation, Transform, Visibility,
    Render, Editor, declared once as SystemStage in core/system.h; app/engine_app.h
    places each system at its stage. */

/** @dir src/engine/system/render
    @brief The engine's whole view of rendering: RenderBackend, RenderView, EditorRenderHooks.

    Deliberately small. Passes are a backend implementation detail and nothing
    above this seam knows one exists. */

/** @dir src/engine/system/physics
    @brief Rigid bodies, collision, joints, ragdolls, character control and queries.

    Deterministic by construction: the solver canonicalises on storage slot, so
    the same world steps the same way twice. */

/** @dir src/engine/system/script
    @brief The gameplay boundary: Behavior, BehaviorContext, and the module that loads a game's code.

    This is the surface a game is actually written against. */

/** @dir src/engine/system/hierarchy
    @brief Parent-child links, and the world transform resolved from them each frame.

    An unconditional pass over a flat array rather than dirty flags: a second
    source of truth about whether a matrix is current is what produces
    invisible-object bugs nobody can reproduce. */

/** @dir src/engine/system/ui
    @brief In-game UI: an ECS-authored tree resolved into one draw list per frame.

    Not the editor's UI, which is ImGui and lives in src/editor. Nothing here
    reaches ImGui at all. */

/** @dir src/engine/core/memory
    @brief The containers everything else is built on: SparseSet, SlotAllocator, TypeRegistry.

    None of them knows what it holds. The Scene pairs the first two to make
    entities and the third to make component storage; the ResourceManager and the
    EventBus pair them differently to make assets and event buses. */

/** @dir src/engine/core/event
    @brief The typed event bus, and the per-type bus it is made of.

    Infrastructure rather than a system: the Engine owns one by value like the
    Clock, and the loop flushes it at the top of the Simulation stage. */

/** @dir src/engine/debug
    @brief Diagnostics: the Tracy profiler facade, the recoverable-error sink, a fault latch and the screenshot writer.

    Engine code includes the profiler facade rather than Tracy. The profiler compiles to
    nothing when it is off; reportError always logs, and also records into the
    editor's error log when a host has installed one. */

/** @dir src/engine/system/animation
    @brief Clips, skeletal posing, bone sockets and the pose buffer a frame publishes.

    A clip is simulation, so its time advances on the tick; the pose it names is
    published on FrameContext for the Visibility and Render stages to read. */

/** @dir src/engine/system/audio
    @brief The mixer seam: voices, spatialization and the one place miniaudio is reached.

    No device is a normal state - the engine runs silently rather than refusing
    to run. Main-thread only; see the reference page before lending it one. */

/** @dir src/engine/system/visibility
    @brief What the camera can see: frustum, distance and screen-size culling.

    A flat parallel sweep rather than a spatial structure, which at this entity
    count has no build cost, no staleness and no second answer about where
    things are. */

/** @dir src/engine/net
    @brief Networking: transport, wire format, replication and client prediction.

    NetSession brackets the frame rather than taking part in it, and offline it
    answers yes to everything - so single-player is unchanged by its existence. */

/** @dir src/engine/platform
    @brief The seam onto the operating system: window, input, threads, shared libraries, sockets. */
